{"author":{"id":"a51b5961cc6de343a64125d8eb6a0f965aa434020b9c46ed932a1bbf143eb532"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1791789678,"nonce":"cx7GcEt3H69AyJcKRTvxkhjsQLA=","message":"Acquisition-path audit (2026-10-12): NativeWindowHost.render reaches surface.acquire after GPU initialization and surface configuration. An invalid view returns before encoder creation, painter/layout work, submission or presentation; dirty remains true and retries keep firing. xgpu surface_acquire currently collapses all non-Success/non-Suboptimal CurrentSurfaceTexture values to handle 0, so the recorded surfaceMisses do not identify Occluded, Timeout, Outdated, Lost or Validation. The pinned wgpu 30.0.1 Metal backend reads the hosting NSWindow.occlusionState directly and returns Occluded before nextDrawable if NSWindowOcclusionStateVisible is absent. This can differ from our event-derived occluded flag, especially before an initial Occluded event. That is a concrete candidate for the no-event failure, but the historical failures do not contain the native acquisition result, so it is not yet proven. Earlier claims that another Space/fullscreen state explains every no-show period were hypotheses. Three bounded normal-level clear-only window probes today all presented their first frame (54-828ms total including initialization, 0/1/1 initial surface misses), so the persistent failure did not reproduce. Next required diagnostic: preserve the acquisition status and correlate it with the native window occlusion state during a failing run; do not treat repeated invalid views alone as proof of an environmental cause.","files":null}]}